{%- comment -%}
  EXAMPLE ONLY — not auto-installed. A developer must adapt the selector(s)
  below to the merchant's actual theme (button markup varies a lot between
  themes) and load this alongside the compiled checkoutBridge.js asset via
  a Theme App Extension app-embed block.

  This snippet does NOT replace Shopify's native checkout button generally
  — it is specifically for the Hub topology described in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
  section C: one storefront ("Hub") showcasing products that each redirect
  to checkout on one of several distinct destination stores (2-10, per
  docs/PHASE_LOG.md's "Correção de Arquitetura — Multi-Loja de Destino").
  Different products on the SAME Hub page can send the customer to
  DIFFERENT destination stores/domains — this snippet is written per-product,
  not per-page, for exactly that reason.

  The mapping of "this product -> that destination store" already exists in
  the merchant's own Shopify data (a product tag, a metafield, or collection
  membership — whichever the merchant already uses to decide where a product
  checks out) and is read here in Liquid, at render time, on the server. This
  snippet never guesses or hardcodes a single destination store for the whole
  theme. The two concrete examples below show a metafield and a tag; use
  whichever the merchant's catalog already has, or adapt to a third scheme.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Example A: destination store recorded as a product metafield, e.g.
  `product.metafields.tracking.destination_shop_id` set to "store-b",
  "store-c", etc. — must match a shop_id registered in the Gateway's
  SHOPIFY_STORES config (apps/gateway/src/config.ts).
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign destination_shop_id = product.metafields.tracking.destination_shop_id | default: "" -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Example B: falls back to reading it off a product tag shaped like
  "destination:store-c", for merchants who prefer tags over metafields.
  Only used if the metafield above wasn't set.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if destination_shop_id == "" -%}
  {%- for tag in product.tags -%}
    {%- if tag contains "destination:" -%}
      {%- assign destination_shop_id = tag | remove: "destination:" -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Each destination store also needs its own fallback checkout URL (fail-open
  path per spec section 50) and its own cart permalink domain — both are
  properties of the destination store, not of the Hub, so they are looked up
  the same way as the shop id above rather than assumed to be a single
  Gateway-wide domain. This example assumes a matching
  `tracking.fallback_checkout_domain` metafield holding e.g.
  "store-c.example.com"; adapt to however the merchant's theme already
  tracks each destination store's domain.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign destination_domain = product.metafields.tracking.fallback_checkout_domain -%}

<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    var buyButton = document.querySelector('[data-buy-on-destination-store]');
    if (!buyButton) return;

    buyButton.addEventListener('click', async function (event) {
      event.preventDefault();

      var cartResponse = await fetch('/cart.js');
      var cart = await cartResponse.json();

      // trackingId/sessionId are the same cookies the Web Pixel
      // (extensions/web-pixel-store-a) maintains — read here via a tiny
      // inline helper rather than duplicating the pixel's cookie logic;
      // see checkoutBridge.ts for the shared TypeScript implementation
      // this example is a plain-JS sketch of.
      var trackingId = window.__trackingPlatform.getTrackingId();
      var sessionId = window.__trackingPlatform.getSessionId();

      await window.__trackingPlatform.initiateTransferAndRedirect({
        trackingId: trackingId,
        sessionId: sessionId,
        cart: cart,
        destinationShopId: buyButton.getAttribute('data-destination-shop-id'),
        appProxyBasePath: '/apps/tracking',
        gatewayPublicUrl: '{{ settings.tracking_gateway_url }}',
        fallbackCheckoutUrl: buyButton.getAttribute('data-fallback-checkout-url'),
      });
    });
  });
</script>

<button
  data-buy-on-destination-store
  data-destination-shop-id="{{ destination_shop_id }}"
  data-fallback-checkout-url="https://{{ destination_domain }}/cart"
>
  Buy now
</button>
